JavaScript Fetch API — GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE
Since you are learning JavaScript API calls and displaying Fake Store API products as cards, let's understand all the important HTTP methods with simple examples and real-world scenarios.
1. What is the Fetch API?
The fetch() method is used to send HTTP requests from JavaScript to a server or API.
For example, when you open an e-commerce website:
- GET — Display products.
- POST — Add a new product.
- PUT — Replace an existing product's details.
- PATCH — Update only selected product details.
- DELETE — Delete a product.

The general syntax is:
fetch(url, {
    method: "GET"
})
.then(response => response.json())
.then(data => {
    console.log(data);
})

fetch() sends the request.
response.json() reads the response body and parses JSON.
.then() handles the result of a successful promise.

GET

Read / Retrieve data
Example: Display all products on a webpage.
fetch("https://fakestoreapi.com/products")
  .then(res => res.json())
  .then(data => console.log(data));





POST

Create data
Example: Add a new product to the store.
fetch("https://fakestoreapi.com/products", {
  method: "POST",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    title: "New T-Shirt",
    price: 499,
    description: "Cotton T-Shirt",
    image: "https://i.pravatar.cc",
    category: "clothing"
  })
})
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));





PUT

Replace / Update a resource
Example: Replace the details of product with ID 1.
fetch("https://fakestoreapi.com/products/1", {
  method: "PUT",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    title: "Updated Laptop",
    price: 55000,
    description: "Updated description",
    image: "https://i.pravatar.cc",
    category: "electronics"
  })
})
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));





PATCH

Update selected fields
Example: Change only the price of product with ID 1.
fetch("https://fakestoreapi.com/products/1", {
  method: "PATCH",
  headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  body: JSON.stringify({
    price: 799
  })
})
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));





DELETE

Delete a resource
Example: Delete product with ID 1.
fetch("https://fakestoreapi.com/products/1", {
  method: "DELETE"
})
.then(res => res.json())
.then(data => console.log(data));

Property	Explanation
method	Specifies GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE.
headers	Provides information about the request, such as the data format.
Content-Type	Tells the server that the request body contains JSON.
body	Contains the data being sent to the server.
JSON.stringify()	Converts a JavaScript object into a JSON string.


In JavaScript, you can convert a JavaScript object into a JSON string using JSON.stringify().
1. JavaScript Object to JSON
const product = {
    title: "New T-Shirt",
    price: 499,
    category: "clothing"
};

const jsonData = JSON.stringify(product);

console.log(jsonData);
console.log(typeof jsonData);


Output:
{"title":"New T-Shirt","price":499,"category":"clothing"}


string


2. Pretty-print JSON
To display JSON in a readable format, use the third parameter 2.
const jsonData = JSON.stringify(product, null, 2);

console.log(jsonData);


Output:
{
  "title": "New T-Shirt",
  "price": 499,
  "category": "clothing"
}


3. JSON String to JavaScript Object
To convert JSON back into a JavaScript object, use JSON.parse().
const jsonString = '{"title":"New T-Shirt","price":499}';

const obj = JSON.parse(jsonString);

console.log(obj.title); // New T-Shirt
console.log(obj.price); // 499
console.log(typeof obj); // object


4. Difference between stringify() and parse()
Method	Conversion	Example
JSON.stringify()	Object → JSON string	Used when sending data to an API
JSON.parse()	JSON string → Object	Used when converting JSON text into an object

The JavaScript fetch() method accepts a URL and an optional options object containing parameters such as method, headers, body and more

1. Complete Fetch API syntax
fetch(url, {
    method: "GET",
    headers: {},
    body: null,
    mode: "cors",
    credentials: "same-origin",
    cache: "default",
    redirect: "follow",
    referrer: "about:client",
    referrerPolicy: "strict-origin-when-cross-origin",
    integrity: "",
    signal: undefined,
    keepalive: false
})
.then(response => {
    return response.json();
})
.then(data => {
    console.log(data);
})
.catch(error => {
    console.error(error);
});


These are the main options, but you generally don't need to specify all of them for every request. Some are browser defaults, and some have restrictions depending on the request.
2. All Fetch parameters explained
1. method
Specifies the HTTP method.
method: "GET"
// GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE





2. headers
Sends metadata about the request, such as content type and authorization.
headers: {
    "Content-Type": "application/json",
    "Authorization": "Bearer YOUR_TOKEN"
}





3. body
Contains the data sent to the server. Commonly used with POST, PUT and PATCH.
body: JSON.stringify({
    title: "Mobile",
    price: 15000
})


For GET requests, a body is generally not allowed.